  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1201 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nAnother \"cooler\" day is on display across the Mid-South with\ncurrent temperatures in the 80s areawide with a little bit of\nagitated cu slowly building in. The latest KNQA radar sweep\nindicates mostly dry conditions across the Mid-South with a few \nvery light showers developing over north Mississippi. Dry \nconditions are not expected to last very long as a moist airmass \nand increased PWATs in the 1.9-2.1\" range remain over areas mainly\nsouth of I- 40. As we move into the afternoon hours, when peak \nheating occurs, mainly diurnally driven isolated to scattered \nshower and thunderstorm development is expected primarily in these\nareas. Today's parameter space looks much like yesterday's with \nPWATs around the 90th percentile for this time of the year and \nenough instability for weak updrafts capable of producing gusty \nwinds and potentially becoming efficient rainfall producers. As a \nshortwave, currently fixated over our area, translates east \nthrough the day, shower and thunderstorm chances will begin to \ntaper off shortly after sunset. As far as temperatures go today, \nhighs will be in the upper 80s to lower 90s with dewpoints in the \nmid 70s, just shy of heat advisory criteria thanks to increased \ncloud cover moving into the afternoon.\n\nTomorrow, however, will be a different story as we clear out\novernight resulting in a warm and humid day tomorrow as ridging\nto our west, currently centered over the four corners region, and\nridging over the southeastern CONUS both begin to broaden. These\nareas of high pressure will shift us, here in the Mid-South, into\nquasi-zonal flow aloft. As this slight upper-level transition\noccurs, we will begin to rapidly warm with dewpoints remaining in\nthe mid 70s. Heat indices will begin to soar to 105+F in areas\nalong and west of the Mississippi River warranting the issuance\nof a Heat Advisory beginning at 11AM tomorrow. We will remain in\nthis pattern through mid-week as temperatures continue to warm\ninto the mid to potentially upper 90s by Wednesday. Continued\nHeat Advisories and potential expansions are anticipated each day\nas ridging continues to broaden. In this typical summertime\npattern, weak mid and upper-level embedded perturbations will\nallow for isolated shower and thunderstorm chances, peaking in\nthe afternoon, each day.\n\nMoving into the latter half of the week, latest guidance has us\ntrending warmer than past runs as ensembles are now keeping dual\nupper-level troughs further north, remaining over Canada. Aloft,\na transition to a northwest flow regime is still expected here\nover Mid-South; however, if troughing remains north, highs in the\nupper 90s even pushing into the weekend are not out of the\nquestion. As such Heat Advisories have the potential to be\nwarranted every day this week and maybe even extending into next\nweek if upper-level ridging is able to hold strong and keep\ntroughing further north. The latest LREF guidance is in agreement\nwith this solution with around a 30% chance of heat indices 105+F\nFriday and into next weekend. Something to keep in mind as we\nhead into mid August. As we transition into more of a northwest\nflow regime, embedded shortwaves will likely give enough lift for\nisolated showers and thunderstorm chances, especially in the\nhours, into late week.\n\nAEH",
